Subject: [PATCH 16/16] x86: the same binding fix patch 14 made for amd64

arch/x86/openbios.c has the identical defect and takes the identical one-call-site
fix: openbios_init() moves to AFTER device_end().

bind_func is is-cfunc (libopenbios/clib.fs:17) is $create, and $create defines
into the CURRENT vocabulary -- so while a device context is open, the words land
in that node rather than the dictionary, invisible to $find.

Measured at the x86 prompt, before and after:

                    before      after
  (init-program)    MISSING     FOUND
  (go)              MISSING     FOUND
  platform-boot     FOUND       FOUND     (already bound after device_end)

The symptom was `load` of any file ending in "Unable to locate (init-program)!"
(forth/debugging/client.fs:57) -- so the whole `load` client path was dead on the
arch that has had CONFIG_LOADER_FORTH set to TRUE all along. After:

    0 > load /ide@1/cdrom@0:\marker.fth
     ok
    0 > load-size . cr
    23
    0 > load-base load-size evaluate
    SPIKE-FORTH-LOADED

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT MOVED, and it is the interesting half. A few lines above
device_end(), x86 binds cif-claim and cif-release:

    push_str("/openprom/client-services");
    fword("find-device");
    bind_func("cif-claim", ciface_claim_x86);
    bind_func("cif-release", ciface_release_x86);

Those are ALSO unreachable to a global $find, and they are SUPPOSED to be: the
find-device wrapper installs them as METHODS OF THAT NODE, which is what ppc and
sparc do for the same two names via NODE_METHODS. Their invisibility is the
mechanism being used correctly, not a second instance of the bug.

That distinction was checked rather than assumed -- an earlier reading of this
same code called them a second defect, on the strength of the probe alone, and
was wrong. The probe answers "is this word global"; it does not answer "should
it be". Only the find-device wrapper does.

Arch coverage for this patch is arch/x86/ only, so no Arch-tested line is
required (tools/check-patch-scope.sh). Run anyway, since the file is load-bearing
for every x86 track: multiboot, dict-identity, nvram, persist and floppy all
PASS, and showcase-rival-boots-linux.sh multiboot PASSes.

STILL OPEN, and new: x86's `go` prints "Evaluating Forth..." and then HANGS,
where amd64's completes and prints the marker. The `evaluate` recipe above works
on both. That divergence is recorded as an UNKNOWN in TODO 13.1 rather than
being smoothed over -- x86 rebases the GDT and carries two definitions of
load-base, either of which could matter, and neither has been measured.

--- a/arch/x86/openbios.c
+++ b/arch/x86/openbios.c
@@ -426,7 +426,6 @@
 static void
 arch_init( void )
 {
-	openbios_init();
 	modules_init();
 #ifdef CONFIG_DRIVER_PCI
 	arch = &default_pci_host;
@@ -475,6 +474,29 @@
 	bind_func("cif-release", ciface_release_x86);
 
 	device_end();
+
